    "B6": {"score": 1, "why": "No README anywhere; three binary columns in the hCONDEL BED whose meaning I could not determine. Scripts recover the rest."},
    "C1": {"score": 2, "why": "Does the released benchmark reproduce the paper's ranking? Informative either way, and I already have the answer."},
    "C2": {"score": 0, "why": "PhyloAcc, phyloP and ForwardGenomics are all open and honestly evaluated; PhyloAcc scores AUROC 0.997-1.000 here."},
    "C3": {"score": 1, "why": "Re-evaluation angle is real and verified, but there is no machine-learning angle once PhyloAcc saturates the task."},
    "C4": {"score": 1, "why": "Paper's own Supplementary Table 3 shows ForwardGenomics at 39-53 GB RAM and PhyloAcc slow; a fast surrogate has some value."},
    "C5": {"score": 2, "why": "Published AUPRC figure plus four released per-locus score sets; I computed the comparison numbers myself."},
    "D1": {"score": 1, "why": "DNABERT-2 and HyenaDNA have open weights and fit 200 bp, but they take single sequences, not alignments."},
    "D2": {"score": 2, "why": "About 84,000 short sequences (6,000 loci x 14 species) embed once on CPU and cache; no GPU needed."},
    "D3": {"score": 2, "why": "DNABERT-2 is Apache-2.0; the data itself is CC BY 4.0, which I read off the FigShare record."},
    "E1": {"ok": true, "why": "Hold out whole locus indices and a whole taxon set; the same 500-locus BED underlies positives and negatives."},
    "E2": {"ok": true, "why": "Six case-by-taxon groups, 500 locus indices, plus two independent real external sets."},
    "E3": {"ok": true, "why": "Balanced 500 positives and 500 negatives per case, so chance precision is exactly 0.5; I computed it."},
    "E4": {"ok": false, "why": "No replicate scores per locus in the release and no MPRA replicate-level data, so no noise floor."},
    "E5": {"ok": true, "why": "Strong positive control verified: PhyloAcc's logbf1 must reproduce AUROC about 0.999, and it does."},
    "E6": {"ok": true, "why": "Label shuffling and feature ablation trivially runnable on a 6,000-row table of 200 bp alignments."},
    "E7": {"ok": true, "why": "Two independent real sets (1,701 hCONDELs, 352 enhancers) plus two independent taxon simulations."},
    "E8": {"ok": true, "why": "Published numbers reproducible; I already recomputed the four-method comparison from the released scores."},
    "G1": {"score": 2, "why": "Verified in the BED: hg38 coordinates with explicit chromosome accessions such as NC_000001.11, plus hCONDEL IDs."},
    "G2": {"score": 2, "why": "Xue et al. Science 2023 table S1 holds MPRA activity for all 10,032 hCONDELs, joinable on hCONDEL ID."},
    "G3": {"score": 1, "why": "ID join is one-to-one but only 1,701 of 10,032 elements have alignments here; six cell types need a collapse rule."},
    "G4": {"score": 2, "why": "Does rate acceleration at a human-specific deletion predict whether it actually changes regulatory activity? Neither set answers alone."},
    "H1": {"score": 2, "why": "Negatives systematic by construction: 500 CaseZ null loci per set and 1,574 non-validated hCONDELs."},
    "H2": {"score": 1, "why": "Two taxon sets plus 10 mammals, but one simulation model with one parameterisation and human-only MPRA data."},
    "H3": {"score": 2, "why": "Claim statable precisely in one sentence; see honest_claim_h3."},
    "H4": {"score": 1, "why": "Paper is honest that real-data performance is only comparable, but does not disclose that released scores fail to reproduce its ranking."},
    "I1": {"score": 2, "why": "Named: the PhyloAcc group (Edwards and Liu labs, Harvard), the CNEwrap authors, and Zoonomia comparative-genomics groups."},
    "I2": {"score": 2, "why": "Would change which acceleration method a lab runs on a new genome set, and whether EvoAcc's extra calls are trusted."},
    "I3": {"score": 1, "why": "AUROC and AUPRC roughly match ranking loci for follow-up; users really want precision in a top-N they would validate."},
    "I4": {"score": 0, "why": "Verified that counting variable sites alone reaches AUROC 0.827-0.997; a one-line rule beats the new algorithm."},
    "J1": {"score": 1, "why": "Mostly parsing alignments and reproducing baselines; little real modelling headroom once the target is saturated."},
    "J2": {"score": 1, "why": "Some intuition: I caught the AUROC 0.003 sign inversion, but phylogenetic rate modelling is not my strength."},
    "J3": {"score": 2, "why": "Minimum shareable version already exists: the re-evaluation table I computed in this session stands alone."},
