        "score_and_split": "Paper claims it beats the other three on simulated data, especially with two or three accelerated lineages. Using the score column its own plotting script uses (ave_score), I measured AUROC 0.371/0.194/0.003 for reptile cases A/B/C and 0.693/0.665/0.016 for ratite A/B/C on balanced 500-vs-500 splits. Its sum_score column does better (0.681-0.839). On real data the paper reports it recovers 34 of 127 validated hCONDELs, the best of the four.",
        "authors_named_unsolved": "They name deeply divergent lineages (for example teleosts) where homologous non-coding elements diverge too much for alignment-based detection, and say alignment-free deep-learning approaches to this still suffer high false-positive rates and poor interpretability.",
