  "integration": {
    "partner_dataset": "Xue et al., Science 2023 table S1: MPRA (massively parallel reporter assay) regulatory activity for all 10,032 human-specific conserved deletions across six cell types, about 800 of them significant (raw reads under SRA PRJNA921914)",
    "join_key": "hCONDEL identifier, with hg38 genomic coordinates as a fallback (present in the BED as explicit chromosome accessions such as NC_000001.11)",
    "bigger_question_one_sentence": "Does evolutionary-rate acceleration at a human-specific deletion predict whether that deletion actually changes regulatory activity in a reporter assay, or are the two largely unrelated?"
  },
  "controls": {
    "positive_control": "PhyloAcc's released logbf1 must reproduce AUROC about 0.999 on the simulated loci (I verified it does, 0.997-1.000), and a trivial variable-site count must separate accelerated from null loci (verified, 0.827-0.997).",
    "negative_control": "Shuffle the accelerated/null label within each case and confirm average precision collapses to 0.5; replace the alignment with random sequence of the same shape; train on the suspected trivial feature (variable-site count) alone to quantify how much is left to learn.",
    "external_validation": "The 1,701 real 10-mammal hCONDEL alignments with 127 MPRA-validated positives, and the 352 HAR/human-gained-enhancer elements with 12 positives; plus training on one taxon set (reptiles) and testing on the other (ratites)."
  },
  "honest_claim_h3": "On alignments generated by this paper's own simulator, a learned model can rank loci by whether a pre-specified clade was given an elevated substitution rate - a task an existing open method (PhyloAcc) already performs at AUROC 0.997-1.000 and a single variable-site count performs at 0.827-0.997 - and transfers to real human-specific deletions only insofar as the 127 reporter-assay-validated elements among 1,701 allow it to be measured at all.",
  "plain_idea": {
    "one_liner": "Use a multi-species DNA alignment to predict whether one branch of the family tree evolved unusually fast.",
    "train_on": "6,000 simulated alignments I counted in the files: 500 loci of 200 base pairs, each generated four ways (one, two or three fast-evolving clades, or none), across three simulation sets of 14-16 species. Each alignment also carries its true simulated slow and fast rates.",
    "predict": "A score for whether a named group of species evolved faster than expected at that locus. The true answer is the parameter the authors' own simulator used to generate the sequence, so it is exact. The separate real-data test uses reporter-assay measurements of regulatory activity from Xue et al., Science 2023.",
    "why_worthwhile": "Comparative-genomics labs must pick one of several acceleration detectors before running it on a new genome set, and have little honest head-to-head evidence. Even a failed model would tell them how much of the signal is just 'count the mismatches'."
  },
  "paper_type": "computational",
  "crowded_field_case": {
    "more_or_better_data": {"answer": "no", "why": "6,000 simulated alignments from a single generating model with one parameter set, and a real benchmark with only 127 positives among 1,701 elements. PhyloAcc was developed and validated on comparable simulations, and PhyloAcc-GT on larger ones."},
    "confidence_score": {"answer": "no", "why": "The release has no replicate scores per locus and no MPRA replicate-level data, so per-prediction noise cannot be estimated. PhyloAcc already reports posterior probabilities and Bayes factors per element, which is exactly a calibrated confidence."},
    "models_beatable": {"answer": "partly", "why": "EvoAcc (2026) is beatable - I already beat its ave_score with a one-line variable-site count on its own benchmark. PhyloAcc is not: its logbf1 reaches AUROC 0.997-1.000 on these same 500-vs-500 balanced splits, leaving no headroom."},
    "guaranteed_improvement": {"answer": "no", "why": "PhyloAcc runs as a baseline and the split is shared, but it already scores about 1.0, so improvement is impossible by construction rather than guaranteed. The joinable new data (MPRA activity) measures regulatory change, not substitution-rate acceleration, so it is a different quantity."}
  },
  "label_elsewhere": {
    "score": 2,
    "sources": "Xue et al., Science 2023 table S1 (MPRA regulatory activity for human-specific conserved deletions, raw reads at SRA PRJNA921914) and Whalen et al., Neuron 2023 (MPRA on human accelerated regions in human and chimpanzee neural progenitors). Both found by search; I did not download either. Note the acceleration label itself exists only in this dataset.",
    "approx_items": 10032,
    "join_key": "hCONDEL identifier, or hg38 genomic coordinates",
    "why": "The reporter-assay label is public for all 10,032 deletions while this release supplies alignments for only 1,701 of them, so the functional half of the question has a ready-made external set six times larger than what is here."
  },
  "leakage_plan": {
    "risks": [
      "The same 500-locus BED underlies all four scenario files, so each locus appears as both a positive (CaseA/B/C) and a negative (CaseZ) carrying the same drawn per-locus rates; a random split would put a locus's positive in training and its own negative in test.",
      "The number of variable sites in an alignment is almost a direct readout of the simulated acceleration - I measured AUROC 0.827-0.997 from that feature alone - so any model will look good for a trivial reason.",
      "Using any released EvoAcc, phyloP, PhyloAcc or ForwardGenomics score as a feature would distil those models rather than learn biology.",
      "On the real set, all 1,701 elements were pre-selected by the authors' own CNE-scanning pipeline, and nearby hCONDELs in the same gene locus are not independent.",
      "Simulated and real alignments differ in length distribution (200 bp fixed versus median 142 bp, range 4-3,320), so length alone could drive apparent transfer success or failure."
    ],
    "split": "Hold out whole locus indices 1-500 across all four scenario files simultaneously, using the elementID in BED column 4 - a column I confirmed is present. Additionally train on one taxon set (reptiles, 14 species) and test on the other (ratites, 16 species). Keep both real sets entirely held out, never trained on.",
    "checks": [
      "Shuffle the accelerated/null label within each case and confirm average precision collapses to 0.5 on the balanced splits.",
      "Fit the variable-site count as a single feature first and report its AUROC as the real baseline, so any model is judged on what it adds beyond mismatch counting.",
      "Check that no locus index appears on both sides of the split, and compare nearest-neighbour sequence similarity between test and training loci.",
      "Confirm no released algorithm score leaked into the feature matrix by training on features alone and on features plus scores and comparing."
    ],
    "verdict": "Manageable, and the main risk is already quantified: the locus-index overlap is easy to fix with a grouped split, but the variable-site shortcut means an impressive number would mean almost nothing."
  },
  "success_criteria": {
    "metric": "AUROC and average precision on a held-out taxon set, reported against the variable-site-count baseline, because the user's decision is which detector to run on a new genome set. For the real transfer test, precision among the top 100 ranked elements, since a lab would validate roughly that many.",
    "baselines": "All measured by me from the released files on balanced 500-vs-500 splits: variable-site count alone AUROC 0.827-0.997; PhyloAcc logbf1 0.997-1.000; phyloP lnlratio 0.568-1.000; ForwardGenomics 0.299-0.932; EvoAcc ave_score 0.003-0.693. On the real set the paper's published counts are EvoAcc 34, phyloP 6, PhyloAcc 4, ForwardGenomics 0 of 127 validated hCONDELs. Base rate there is 127 of 1,701, about 7.5%.",
    "success": "Beat the variable-site count on a held-out taxon set by a clear margin and reach precision above 25% in the top 100 of the 1,701 real elements, against the 7.5% base rate and against EvoAcc's 34 of 127.",
    "noise_ceiling": null,
    "kill": "If a model cannot beat the variable-site count on the held-out taxon set within one week, stop - the simulated task is already saturated by PhyloAcc at 0.997-1.000 and there is nothing to win."
  },
  "publication_target": {
    "ambitious": {"journal": "Molecular Biology and Evolution", "why": "This is where PhyloAcc and the simulation-benchmark literature for rate-shift detection live; it would take a genuinely better detector than PhyloAcc, which my measurements say is not available here."},
    "realistic": {"journal": "Bioinformatics (Applications Note or Letter)", "why": "Publishes method benchmarks and re-evaluations; a careful head-to-head on the authors' own released simulated scores, showing a variable-site count beats the new algorithm while PhyloAcc reaches 0.997-1.000, fits a short note."},
    "fallback": {"journal": "NAR Genomics and Bioinformatics, or a bioRxiv preprint", "why": "Publishes negative and partial benchmark results, so the re-analysis is shareable even if no new model is built and the discrepancy turns out to be a score-convention problem."}
  },
  "pitch": {
    "headline": "The authors released every per-locus score behind their benchmark, so their central claim can be checked in an afternoon - and it does not hold up.",
    "what_makes_the_data_unusual": "6,000 simulated 200 bp alignments with exact ground truth, plus all four competing algorithms' per-locus scores, 18 MB, CC BY 4.0. Every count in the paper matched the files exactly: 10,032 hCONDELs, 803 validated, 1,701 mapped, 127 both.",
    "the_win": "Already computed: PhyloAcc reaches AUROC 0.997-1.000 on all six case-by-taxon splits while EvoAcc's own ave_score gives 0.003-0.693, and a bare variable-site count gives 0.827-0.997.",
    "even_if_it_fails": "If the discrepancy is just a score-direction convention or a stale archive, the honest answer is that nothing new is learned beyond a caveat for CNEwrap users - and no machine-learning result is reachable either way, because PhyloAcc already sits at 1.0.",
    "who_wants_it": "Comparative-genomics groups choosing an acceleration detector (the PhyloAcc group at Harvard, Zoonomia-style consortia) and the CNEwrap authors themselves, who would want to correct or clarify the released scores."
  },
  "ml_idea": "Train a small model (gradient-boosted trees on alignment features, or a frozen DNABERT-2 embedding per species row with a learned head) on the 6,000 simulated 200 bp multi-species alignments to score whether a pre-specified clade evolved at an elevated substitution rate, using the simulator's own generating parameters as ground truth. Benchmark against all four released algorithms on identical loci with a grouped split by locus index and by taxon set, then test transfer to the 1,701 real 10-mammal human-deletion alignments with 127 reporter-assay-validated positives. In practice the modelling half is close to dead on arrival: PhyloAcc already reaches AUROC 0.997-1.000 on the simulated target and a single variable-site count reaches 0.827-0.997, so the defensible deliverable is the honest re-evaluation rather than a new model.",
  "effort": {
    "hours_to_feature_table": 8,
    "working_set_gb": 0.1,
    "needs_gpu": false,
    "peak_ram_gb": 2,
    "embedding_hours": 4,
    "cv_run_minutes": 5,
    "compute_note": "Estimated, not timed, except that I actually parsed every table and computed the full four-method comparison in this session in under a minute on CPU. Embedding estimate assumes 84,000 sequences of 200 bp through a small DNA language model on a laptop CPU.",
    "cheapest_killing_check": "Compute AUROC of the raw variable-site count against the accelerated/null label on the simulated sets. I did it: 0.827-0.997 across all six case-by-taxon combinations, which kills the machine-learning headroom before any model is written."
  },
  "verdict": "park",
  "verdict_reason": "Part A passes cleanly and this is the rare paper whose every released count matched the files exactly, but the machine-learning target is already saturated: open PhyloAcc reaches AUROC 0.997-1.000 on the authors' own simulated benchmark and a one-line variable-site count reaches 0.827-0.997, so there is no modelling headroom (C2 = 0, I4 = 0, model-readiness 7 of 12, finishability 5 of 10). What I did find is worth keeping: using the exact score column the paper's own plotting script uses, EvoAcc's released per-locus scores give AUROC 0.003-0.693 and no single sign convention repairs them, which contradicts the paper's central claim. Park it as a short reproducibility note - first confirm the discrepancy is not a stale archive or a score-direction convention by opening scripts_for_figures.7z (52.8 MB) - rather than as an ML project."
}
